<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="NADH repair (prokaryotes)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>Studies in the 1950s have shown that |FRAME: GAPOXNPHOSPHN-RXN &quot;glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase&quot;| slowly catalyzes the formation of a hydrated form of NADH, called |FRAME: NADHX|, from NADH |CITS: [Rafter54]|. The equilibrium of this hydration reaction is in favor (100/1) of the hydrated form |CITS: [3061454]|, making the reaction virtually irreversible. In addition, NADHX can be formed spontaneously from NADH under slightly acidic conditions. NADHX and NADPHX are inhibitors of several dehydrogenases |CITS: [ 9851831]| and it is thus important to eliminate them. This is achieved by a bifunctional enzyme. One of its activities, that of |FRAME: EC-4.2.1.136|, converts |FRAME: CPD-653| back to |FRAME: NADH|. Since both |FRAME: CPD0-2472| and |FRAME: CPD-653| are formed, but the enzyme can only dehydrate the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-form, the enzyme possesses a second activity, that of |FRAME: EC-5.1.99.6|, which epimerizes the &lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt; form to the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-form |CITS: [21994945]|. In eukaryotes the two activities are catalyzed by two different enzymes. The dehydratase is ATP-dependent, unlike the ADP-dependent prokaryotic enzyme (see |FRAME: PWY-6938|).</Description>
